<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ui version="4.0">
 <class>GaussianSplatSettingsWidget</class>
 <widget class="QWidget" name="GaussianSplatSettingsWidget">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>340</width>
    <height>440</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <property name="windowTitle">
   <string>Form</string>
  </property>
  <layout class="QFormLayout" name="formLayout">
   <item row="0" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="preProcessingSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Pre-processing</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Rebuilds the cloud or its LoD tree. Seconds of work on a large capture, and the result is what every frame afterwards draws.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="1" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lodBaseLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>LoD base</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Voxel-grid growth factor between tree levels, used when a splat object's LoD tree is built (requires reloading/re-adding the splat object to take effect - doesn't affect already-built trees).</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="1" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="lodBaseDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Voxel-grid growth factor between tree levels, used when a splat object's LoD tree is built (requires reloading/re-adding the splat object to take effect - doesn't affect already-built trees).</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>1.01000000000000001</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>4.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="2" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="mergeLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Merge:</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How alike two splats have to be before "Merge coplanar" will treat them as the same surface seen twice. Colour is the allowed difference per channel (0-1); angle is the allowed difference in facing, in degrees. Loosening either shows up as colour bleeding and as surfaces thickening - a disc tilted by the angle tolerance contributes that much of its own radius across the surface it is merged onto, which is what makes the merged cloud less flat. Measured acceptable setting on the reference capture: 0.2 and 20.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="2" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeParamsWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeParamsHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeColourTolDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Allowed difference in colour per channel, on the 0-1 scale. Loosening it merges splats that do not look alike, which shows up as colour bleeding across a surface. Measured acceptable setting on the reference capture: 0.2.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>colour </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.001000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeAngleTolDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Allowed difference in facing, in degrees. This is the knob that decides how flat the merged cloud stays: a disc tilted by this much contributes about 0.7 of that tilt as thickness across the surface it is merged onto, and no amount of flattening removes it, because it is the member's own shape rather than its position. Measured acceptable setting: 20.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>angle </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> deg</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>90.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>26.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeAcrossDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Reach along the surface, in centimetres of world space. This is the whole prize and the whole cost. Measured on the reference capture: 1 cm saves about 5% of fill, 2 cm 13%, 4 cm 34% - and the picture visibly softens from 2 cm up, because a group this wide is replaced by one splat that has to cover all of it. Accepted setting: 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>across </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> cm</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeThroughDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Reach at right angles to the surface, in centimetres of world space - how deep a stack the merge will collapse. Nearly free in quality, and nearly free in saving too: measured, it saturates by about 10 cm and buys nothing past it (5 to 40 cm moved fill from 3.3% to 5.2%, and the largest group found did not grow at all), because a captured surface is a thin shell rather than a deep stack. Large values also get very slow. Accepted setting: 10.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>through </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> cm</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="3" column="0">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeButtonsWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeButtonsHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="mergeCoplanarPushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Merge coplanar</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Collapses groups of near-duplicate splats - same place, same facing, same colour, within the reach and tolerances above - into one splat each, and rebuilds the LoD trees. A group is only collapsed if the replacement paints less than the members did, so a capture without real stacking is mostly left alone rather than being made worse. Applies to every splat object in the world, takes seconds on a large capture, and does not touch the files: 'Restore unmerged' puts it back. Take a frustum report before and after from the same spot - the "blended" line and the layers-to-saturation figure are the answer, splat count is not.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="restoreUnmergedPushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Restore unmerged</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Puts every merged cloud back to the splats it was loaded with, so the merged and unmerged pictures can be compared from a fixed camera.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="3" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeTailWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeTailHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="mergeFlattenCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Flatten</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Collapse each merged group onto the surface it lies on, instead of fitting a splat around where its members actually sat. Without this, the depth of a stack ends up as the thickness of its replacement - a 20 cm deep stack of flat discs becomes a 20 cm thick lens, which costs nothing seen face-on but stops looking like a surface, and is what makes a generous 'through' go soft. With it, the replacement stays as thin as its members and only their sideways spread widens it. Unchecked reproduces the earlier behaviour, for comparison.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QLabel" name="mergeCoplanarResultLabel">
        <property name="text">
         <string></string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="4" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QFrame" name="realTimeSeparatorLine">
     <property name="frameShape">
      <enum>QFrame::HLine</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="frameShadow">
      <enum>QFrame::Sunken</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="5" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="realTimeSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Real time</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Applied to the frame being drawn now. A change here costs nothing but the next traversal.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="6" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="pixelScaleLimitLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Pixel scale limit</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Traversal stops refining a node once its on-screen footprint is below this many pixels. Lower = more detail, more splats drawn. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="6" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="pixelScaleLimitDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Traversal stops refining a node once its on-screen footprint is below this many pixels. Lower = more detail, more splats drawn. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>50.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="7" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="alphaAdjustLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha adjustments</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Rescales every splat's own opacity before anything else looks at it: alpha' = gain * alpha^gamma, clamped to 1. Applied at the earliest point that can still be live - in the vertex shader, before the quad is sized - so it moves the drawn area as well as the pixel's colour, and everything downstream (the saturation gate, the overdraw views, the frustum report) sees the adjusted value. Both at 1 is the cloud as captured. Note it does not reach the LoD tree, which was built from the stored alpha; rebuild the tree to fold a setting in permanently.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="7" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="alphaAdjustWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="alphaAdjustHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaGainDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Multiplies every splat's opacity, after the gamma below. Above 1 makes the cloud denser - fewer layers are needed to cover a pixel, so the saturation gate finishes pixels sooner, but quads also grow, since a splat is drawn out to where its alpha decays to the alpha cutoff. Below 1 thins it out. 1 = as captured.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gain </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaGammaDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Exponent applied to opacity before the gain: alpha^gamma. Below 1 lifts the faint splats towards the opaque ones (the low-opacity bulk the fill sits in); above 1 pushes them down and leaves only what was already solid. This is the knob that changes the shape of the opacity distribution rather than just its scale - see the "Fill by opacity" histogram in the frustum report. 1 = as captured.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gamma </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="alphaAdjustIgnoreCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>ignore</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Sends gain and gamma as 1 and 1 without touching what the boxes say, so a setting can be switched in and out against the same camera. Not remembered between sessions: it is a comparison, not a preference.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="8" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="alphaCutoffLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha cutoff</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-splat quad radius is cut to exactly where its alpha decays to this value, instead of a fixed 3-sigma bound - lossless at the default (1/255 = 0.0039, matches the fragment shader's own discard), trims low-opacity splats' quads further above that (less overdraw, at the cost of their faintest edge). Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="8" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaCutoffDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-splat quad radius is cut to exactly where its alpha decays to this value, instead of a fixed 3-sigma bound - lossless at the default (1/255 = 0.0039, matches the fragment shader's own discard), trims low-opacity splats' quads further above that (less overdraw, at the cost of their faintest edge). Applies live.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>4</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.000100000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>0.500000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.001000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>0.003900000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="9" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="maxSplatsBudgetLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Max splats budget</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-cloud cap on how many LoD tree nodes one traversal may select. If a cloud's diagnostics show "!WARNING! budget cap", this is limiting detail on that cloud even though pixel_scale_limit hasn't converged. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="9" column="1">
    <widget class="QSpinBox" name="maxSplatsBudgetSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-cloud cap on how many LoD tree nodes one traversal may select. If a cloud's diagnostics show "!WARNING! budget cap", this is limiting detail on that cloud even though pixel_scale_limit hasn't converged. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <number>1000</number>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <number>999999999</number>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <number>100000</number>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="resortMoveThresholdLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Resort move threshold (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How far the camera has to move (scaled by distance to the cloud) before a cloud's LoD frontier is re-traversed. Lower = frontier updates more often, at more CPU cost. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="resortMoveThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How far the camera has to move (scaled by distance to the cloud) before a cloud's LoD frontier is re-traversed. Lower = frontier updates more often, at more CPU cost. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.01000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>100.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.01000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lodCapsLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>LoD caps</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Two ceilings on how far LoD traversal may unfold, both independent of pixel scale limit, both 0 = disabled. Density: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (the same layer-count scale the debug ramp uses) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further - find a problem area's layer count with the debug view, then set this a bit below it. Depth: a hard global ceiling on levels from the tree root, everywhere in the world. Both apply live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="lodCapsWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="lodCapsHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="maxLayerDensityDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>density </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Caps LoD traversal: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (see the "Show overdraw" ramp above - the same layer-count scale) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further, regardless of pixel scale limit. Use the overdraw view to find a problem area's layer count, then set this a bit below it. 0 = disabled (no effect on the visualisation above - only on real rendering). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="maxTreeDepthSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>depth </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Hard global ceiling on how many levels traversal may unfold from the tree root, everywhere in the world - independent of pixel scale limit / max layer density / max splats budget. A node at this depth is always kept as its own merged approximation, never refined further. 0 = disabled (no ceiling). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1000</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="numDrawSlicesLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Draw slices</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="drawSlicesWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="drawSlicesHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="numDrawSlicesSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1024</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="visibleSlicingCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>by visible</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Places the slice boundaries so each slice holds an equal share of the splats actually in frustum, instead of an equal share of the draw order. The draw order is sorted by distance and covers the whole cloud including what is behind you, so the nearest splats by distance are a sphere around the camera - standing above a forest looking into the distance, the first slices are almost all ground below and behind, and the saturation census after each of them photographs a mask that is still completely black. Nothing is culled and the whole order is still drawn: only where it is cut moves, so this cannot change the picture, only where the censuses happen. Costs a few thousand point-in-frustum tests per cloud per frame, whatever the cloud's size. Off = the plain index split, for A/B against the same camera.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="drawSliceLimitSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Diagnostic: stops after this many slices, so the frame is deliberately incomplete - with 6 slices and a limit of 3, only the nearest half of every cloud is drawn. Raise it one step at a time and watch the splat pass time: the step that costs the most is the depth range the fill is actually in. 0 = no limit, the whole cloud, which is the only setting the picture is correct at.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>limit </string>
        </property>
        <property name="specialValueText">
         <string>limit off</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1024</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sliceGrowthLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Slice growth</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sliceGrowthDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.250000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>16.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.050000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>1.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="saturationGateCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Saturation gate</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Skips splats at pixels the front-to-back composite has already finished with: between draw slices, pixels whose accumulated coverage has reached the threshold are marked, and later slices are rejected there before being shaded. Needs more than one draw slice to do anything. Off in the overdraw views, where the accumulated alpha counts layers rather than coverage.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="saturationWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="saturationHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="saturationThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>at </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Accumulated coverage at or above which a pixel counts as finished. 0.9961 (= 1 - 1/255) means the light still getting through is under one 8-bit level, which is where the reference 3DGS rasteriser stops. Lowering it skips more work and eventually shows. The accumulation buffer is 16-bit float, whose steps near 1.0 are about 0.0005 wide, so values closer to 1 than that make no difference.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.005000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="saturationMaskDownscaleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>mask 1/</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>How many screen pixels across one texel of the saturation gate's mask covers. 1 = a full-resolution mask, 4 = a mask with a sixteenth of the pixels. A texel is marked only when every pixel under it is finished, so coarsening the mask never skips a pixel that was still being drawn - the only loss is the partly finished texels around the edge of a saturated region. Making the mask small is what makes checking often affordable. Rebuilds the mask when changed.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>16</number>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coverageShrinkStrengthDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>shrink </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Diagnostic: instead of the gate's binary keep/drop, shrinks a splat's quad by (1 - coverage * this), where coverage is the mean accumulated coverage already under it (a continuous estimate, read from a mean pyramid built alongside the gate's own). 0 (default, off): no shrink. 1: a splat over an already-fully-covered pixel shrinks to nothing; 0.5 shrinks it by at most half. Requires the gate above to be on - it reads the coverage the gate's own mark pass already computed rather than paying for a pass of its own.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="layerCapLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Layer cap</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: stop drawing into a pixel once this many splats have blended into it. Counts the layers once for the camera you are standing at, marks the pixels that reached the cap, and then drops splats standing there before any of their work is done - so it costs one counting pass when the camera moves and nothing on the frames after it. The grain is a whole splat, not a pixel: with the conservative test a splat is dropped only where every pixel under it already has its layers, so no hole can open and pixels near the edge of a dense region keep more than the cap. 0 = no cap.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="layerCapWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="layerCapHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="layerCapSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layers to keep per pixel, nearest first. 0 = no cap. Yields to Clip below when that is ticked - there is only one mask, and an explicit diagnostic wins over this one.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="specialValueText">
         <string>cap off</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>255</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="layerCapOpaqueCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>opaque</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>What a pixel the cap cut short is composited as. Ticked: fully covered, keeping the colour its kept layers averaged to, so the cut shows as a colour error. Unticked: as covered as those layers made it, so the background shows through wherever the cap bit - the quickest way to see where it bit at all.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="layerCapEstimatePushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Estimate</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Measures what a cap at this depth would remove, without capping anything: the frame it runs in draws uncapped, its per-pixel layer counts are read back, and the answer is the share of blended fragments sitting deeper than the cap. That is the ceiling any mechanism cutting at this depth could reach, free of what the mechanism itself would cost. Stalls for a moment; the result appears beside it and in the log.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="layerCapOnCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>on</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Applies the cap beside it, without having to zero the value to switch it off - for A/B against the same camera. Unticked sends no cap at all, so the frame is exactly what it would be without this diagnostic. Not remembered between sessions.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="hideTestComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which test drops a splat against the mask - applies to the layer cap and to Clip alike. Conservative: only when the splat's whole quad is inside the marked region, so nothing that could still have shown is lost and the picture keeps at least the capped number of layers everywhere. Centre: when the texel under the splat's centre is marked, which cuts through the region and leaves holes - not a picture, but the way to see how much that region was carrying. Quads are bigger than the connected marked patches, so conservative removes far fewer splats.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>conservative</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>centre (holes)</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="16" column="1">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="layerCapEstimateResultLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string></string>
     </property>
     <property name="wordWrap">
      <bool>true</bool>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="17" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="coverageCapLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha saturation cap</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: the same per-fragment cut as the layer cap above, but decided by how covered a pixel already is rather than by how many splats have touched it. Front-to-back compositing means a pixel whose accumulated coverage has reached the threshold cannot be changed by anything drawn behind it, so everything still to come there is invisible and can be dropped. Unlike the layer cap this needs no per-pixel counter buffer, which is 8 bytes per fragment of writes the pass no longer pays. Takes the saturation mask over from the gate while it is on - there is only one mask.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="17" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="coverageCapWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="coverageCapHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coverageCapThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Accumulated coverage at or above which a pixel stops being composited. Coverage is not a layer count: 1 unit of summed alpha is 63% covered, 2 is 86%, 3 is 95%, 5.5 is 99.6% - so a threshold near 1 is a much later stop than it looks. Separate from the saturation gate's threshold on purpose, so switching between the two does not retune the other. Cuts at slice boundaries, so it needs more than one draw slice.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.005000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.950000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="coverageCapOnCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>on</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Applies the cap beside it, without having to clear the value to switch it off - for A/B against the same camera. Yields to the layer cap above when that is on, and stands the saturation gate down while it runs, since all three want the same mask. Not remembered between sessions.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="18" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="ablationStageLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Ablation stage</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: switches the splat pass on one piece at a time, lightest first, so the frame time can be read at each step. The difference between two steps is what the one thing that step added costs. Read the "draw splats" timer, not the frame rate - vsync hides everything. Two caveats: GPU cost is a maximum over the units that saturate rather than a sum of stages, so a step means "the cost of this given everything below it"; and at the CPU-only stage the splat timer reads nothing, so look at CPU frame time there instead.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="18" column="1">
    <widget class="QComboBox" name="ablationStageComboBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Off is the real pass. The stages below it are deliberately wrong pictures - each one is missing everything above it - so nothing here is meant to be left on. Stage 5 is there because going straight from points to full quads moves the vertex maths and the fill at the same time: it runs all of the projection maths and still draws a one-pixel quad, so 5 minus 4 is the vertex work alone and 6 minus 5 is the fill alone.</string>
     </property>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>off (full pass)</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>1 - CPU only, no draw call</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>2 - points, 1 texel, white</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>3 - points, all 4 texels</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>4 - points, splat colour</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>5 - vertex maths, 1px quad</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>6 - full quads, opaque, no blend</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>7 - blending, flat alpha</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>8 - Gaussian, no discard</string>
      </property>
     </item>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="quadRadiusScaleLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Quad radius scale</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: multiplies every splat's screen-space radius. 1 is the real size. Rasterised area goes as the square of it, while the splat count, all the vertex work and the length of the blend chain stay exactly the same - so this asks whether a cost is proportional to area at all. A cost that falls by half at 0.7 is fill (rasterising, shading, blend bandwidth); one that does not move is per-splat (instance emission, primitive setup), whatever the stage ladder suggests. Not the same as raising the alpha cutoff, which shrinks each splat by its own opacity instead of scaling them all alike. The Gaussian shrinks with the quad, so splats get smaller rather than hard-edged.&#10;&#10;The two boxes beside it (gamma, ref) make the reduction area-weighted instead of flat: splats at or above the reference radius get the full reduction, smaller ones a tapering fraction of it - see their own tooltips. Both inert while this is 1.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="quadRadiusScaleWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="quadRadiusScaleHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="quadRadiusScaleDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>1 = the real size, and the only value the picture is correct at.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="areaScaleGammaDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Activation speed: how sharply the reduction above concentrates on splats bigger than the reference radius beside it, instead of being applied flatly to every splat regardless of size. 1 = weight is linear in the splat's own screen-space area. Above 1, smaller splats are increasingly spared and the reduction concentrates on the biggest ones; below 1, a partial reduction spreads onto smaller splats too. Inert while Quad radius scale is 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gamma </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>8.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="areaScaleRefPxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Reference radius, in screen pixels (before either scale). Splats at or above it get the full Quad radius scale reduction; smaller ones get a fraction of it, tapering off at the rate gamma sets. Inert while Quad radius scale is 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>ref </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> px</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="20" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="accumBuffer8BitCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>8-bit accumulation buffer</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Blends the splats into an RGBA8 buffer instead of the default RGBA16F, halving the bytes each fragment writes. The splat pass is mostly bound by blend bandwidth, so this is worth about a third of it. The cost is precision: hundreds of blends accumulate per pixel and the resolve then divides the accumulated colour by the accumulated coverage, so quantisation is amplified where coverage is low - cloud silhouettes and thin sparse regions, not the dense areas. It also weakens the saturation gate, whose saving is the blending it avoids. Rebuilds the accumulation framebuffer when toggled.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="20" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QFrame" name="diagnosticsSeparatorLine">
     <property name="frameShape">
      <enum>QFrame::HLine</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="frameShadow">
      <enum>QFrame::Sunken</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="21" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="diagnosticsSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Diagnostics</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Measures or exposes what is there. Nothing here is meant to be left on.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="22" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sizeClampLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Size clamp min/max (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Debug tool for locating abnormally large/degenerate splats (e.g. under-reconstructed scene edges). Hides any splat whose feature size (2 x its largest scale axis, in metres) falls outside [min, max]. 0 disables that bound (same "0 = unlimited" convention as the Splats list search radius) - default (0, 0) has no effect. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="22" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="sizeClampWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="sizeClampHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sizeClampMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Splats with feature size below this (metres) are hidden. Applies live. 0 = no lower cull.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sizeClampMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Splats with feature size above this (metres) are hidden. Applies live. 0 (default) = no upper cull.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="sizeClampInvertCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Invert</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Unchecked (default): hide splats outside [min, max] - isolate a size range. Checked: hide splats inside [min, max] instead - exclude a size range, leaving the rest of the cloud untouched. No effect while both bounds are 0.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="23" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="distClampLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Distance slice min/max (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Slices the cloud open by distance from the camera, so you can look inside it a shell at a time. Shows only splats whose distance from the camera lies inside [min, max] metres; tick Invert to hide that shell instead and see through it. The distance is measured from the camera position, so the slice is a spherical shell and turning on the spot does not change what survives. Default (0, 1000) shows everything. Applies live, in the vertex shader - no re-traversal, and free when left wide open.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="23" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="distClampWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="distClampHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="distClampInvertCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Invert</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Unchecked (default): show only what is inside [min, max]. Checked: hide that shell and show everything outside it - which is how you cut away the near wall and look into the room behind it.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="24" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="showDebugCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Show debug</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Replaces splat colour with a flat additive increment per surviving fragment, so the picture burns out where many splats pile onto the same pixel. What is accumulated depends on the mode beside this box, and the two answer different questions:&#10;&#10;Overdraw - sums 1.0 per fragment, giving depth complexity: how many splats a pixel has to blend. That is what drives blend cost, rather than raw splat count.&#10;&#10;Alpha - sums each fragment's alpha instead, so the ramp shows accumulated alpha per pixel. Read against the layer count from the same spot it sizes up a front-to-back transmittance cutoff, the early-out a hardware blend pipeline cannot do per fragment: the share of fragment work such a cutoff could skip is roughly (summed alpha) / 5.5. Hundreds of layers beside a small alpha sum means near-threshold splats a cutoff would never reach - that case needs pruning, not early-out.&#10;&#10;Alpha sums are far smaller numbers than layer counts, so set the range below to match the mode. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="24" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="debugModeWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="debugModeHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="debugModeComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which measure the debug ramp and Clip work on. See the tooltip on "Show debug" for what each one answers.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Overdraw</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Alpha</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="clipCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Clip</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Throws away every splat standing where the chosen measure has reached the range maximum - exactly the region the ramp paints solid red - and draws what is left, so "are the red regions really where the time goes" can be answered by removing them and looking at the clock instead of by inference. Follows the mode beside it: layers with Overdraw selected, accumulated alpha with Alpha.&#10;&#10;The splats are dropped in the vertex shader, before rasterisation. It costs an extra counting pass over the splats, so the frame total is meaningless while this is on - read the "draw splats" GPU timer, which starts after that pass and so measures the same thing in both states. Switches the saturation gate off while it is on; they share the same mask.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="25" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="overdrawRangeLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Overdraw range (layers)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Layer-count range the overdraw ramp above maps to blue (min) .. green (midpoint) .. red (max). E.g. [2, 100]: pixels with 2 or fewer overlapping splats are solid blue, 100+ are solid red, in between fades smoothly. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="25" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="overdrawRangeWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="overdrawRangeHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="overdrawRangeMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layer count that maps to solid blue.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>2.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="overdrawRangeMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layer count that maps to solid red.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <!-- The four one-off diagnostic actions sit together in a single row at the foot of the panel, rather than
   each taking a form row of its own: they are not settings, and interleaving them with the live controls above
   made the panel taller than the dock for no gain.  Their result labels follow in the row below, same order. -->
   <item row="26" column="0" colspan="2">
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="diagnosticButtonsHorizontalLayout">
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="countInFrustumPushButton">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Count in frustum</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>One-off count (not live) of splats that are both in the camera's current view frustum and pass the size clamp above (respecting Invert). If the clamp is disabled (0/0), counts every in-frustum splat.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="frustumReportPushButton">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Frustum report</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>One-off structure report (not live), written to the log: for every cloud in view, how much of the LoD tree is unfolded at this camera position, and for each drawn node why the traversal stopped there - converged, capped by density/depth/budget, or a leaf with nothing coarser above it. Also the trees' depth and branching, and the layer_density of what is being drawn. Runs a full traversal per cloud on the main thread, so expect a hitch on a large cloud.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="resetImportancePushButton">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Reset importance</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Forgets the per-splat importance the frustum report has been accumulating, so the next report starts a fresh set of viewpoints. Every report press folds its viewpoint into a running record, which is what lets it say how much of the cloud earns nothing from any of the places you have stood - the single-camera figure cannot, since a splat hidden from here may be the front layer from there. Press this before starting a deliberate tour, and after any report taken from a viewpoint you did not mean to include.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="saturationSnapshotsPushButton">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Saturation snapshots</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Diagnostic dump, not a rendering setting: writes the next frame's splat pass to disk as PNGs - the running composite, its coverage and what each slice alone added, plus every level of the saturation mask as it stood between slices. For looking at where the fill actually lands, layer by layer, and at how much of the mask survives being read conservatively. Needs the saturation gate on and more than one slice, since it is the gate's censuses being photographed; the press is ignored otherwise. The captured frame does a full-screen readback per slice, so it takes seconds and its timings mean nothing.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item row="27" column="0" colspan="2">
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="diagnosticResultsHorizontalLayout">
     <item>
      <widget class="QLabel" name="countInFrustumResultLabel">
       <property name="text">
        <string></string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QLabel" name="frustumReportResultLabel">
       <property name="text">
        <string></string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QLabel" name="resetImportanceResultLabel">
       <property name="text">
        <string></string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QLabel" name="saturationSnapshotsResultLabel">
       <property name="text">
        <string></string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item row="28" column="0" colspan="2">
    <spacer name="verticalSpacer">
     <property name="orientation">
      <enum>Qt::Vertical</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0">
      <size>
       <width>20</width>
       <height>40</height>
      </size>
     </property>
    </spacer>
   </item>
  </layout>
 </widget>
